WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.681
For all the new faces on the screen, many
of Solo's characters are old mainstays in

00:00:04.681 --> 00:00:07.660
the Star Wars universe with their destinies
already laid out.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.160
We know Lando betrays Han on Cloud City.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.640
We know Han eventually meets Leia, has a kid,
and gets light-stabbed.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.820
And we know Chewbacca hates Porgs.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.820
But what about the other characters in Solo:
A Star Wars Story?

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:23.440
True to form, the movie is a menagerie of
aliens, droids, and rebel scum, and believe

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.080
it or not, a few of those characters come
into play in other Star Wars stories.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:31.820
Here are all the characters in Solo: A Star
Wars Story with more meaning than you realized.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.700
Spoilers ahead, obviously.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:35.620
L3-37

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.120
Ever since the beginning, the Millennium Falcon
has seemed to have a mind of its own.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.020
Way back in The Empire Strikes Back, C-3PO
mentions that the Falcon has "the most peculiar

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.180
dialect" after trying to communicate with
it.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:51.579
And now we know why, thanks to a new character
who's apparently been in all the main Star

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:54.980
Wars films: L3-37, Lando's activist droid.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:57.060
By herself, she's a unique piece of work.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.270
As Solo co-writer Jon Kasdan explained;

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:00.980
"She’s a self-modified droid.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.940
The idea is that she’s sort of a mutt, if
you will, of various parts of different kinds

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.000
of droids who has improved upon herself."

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.140
But when Han, Lando, Chewie, and Qi'ra are
trying to navigate the Maelstrom, they have

00:01:11.141 --> 00:01:15.130
no choice but to plug the defunct L3's database
into the Falcon's computer.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:19.980
And, as far as we can tell, they leave it
there, giving the ship L3-37's thoughts and

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:20.480
sassiness.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:22.020
Weazel

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.780
By now, most people know that Solo: A Star
Wars Story features a surprise cameo by a

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:30.030
character from The Phantom Menace, and we'll
get to that in a moment.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.490
But what you may not have realized is that
there were actually two Phantom characters

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:34.780
on-screen in Solo.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.430
The character of Weazel, played by Star Wars
mainstay Warwick Davis, is one of Enfys Nest's

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:40.560
marauders in Solo.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:44.640
As it turns out, he's taken a long journey
to end up as one of Nest's rebels.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.630
In The Phantom Menace, Weazel - again played
by Davis - appears as one of the spectators

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:49.700
of Anakin's pod race.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.920
He even bets on Anakin to come in first.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:56.420
The Official Guide to Solo: A Star Wars Story
seems to confirm him as the same character,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.540
saying that he was once a petty thief who
worked for Jabba the Hutt.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.220
What's he been up to in the meantime?

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.060
Probably regretting that bet he made on Anakin,
since he turned out to be a Sith Lord and

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:05.560
all.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:06.980
Cloud-Riders

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.120
One of the best additions to Solo was Enfys
Nest and her band of Cloud-Riders, a pirate

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.800
gang that seemed to show up at every job Han
pulled.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.860
As it turns out, these guys hark back to the
Marvel line of comics as well.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.260
In Star Wars #9, Han is called out to a planet
to stop the Cloud-Riders' raids on a local

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:23.760
village.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.840
There, he goes blaster-to-blaster with the
airborne marauders.

00:02:26.849 --> 00:02:30.760
The story is obviously different from what
happened in Solo, but co-writer Jon Kasdan

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.900
confirmed that the name for the Cloud-Riders
came directly from that comic issue, along

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.260
with the design ideas for the gang's swoop
bikes.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.680
With this as a precedent, who knows what other
non-canon expanded universe concepts could

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.100
still make it to the big screen?

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:44.680
Darth Maul

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:50.120
Solo had a big surprise waiting near the end
in the form of Darth Maul, who you may remember

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:55.140
as the red-faced Sith got a free case of the
halvsies courtesy of Obi-Wan in The Phantom

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:55.640
Menace.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:59.980
"This was a particularly bad case of somebody
being cut in half."

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:01.880
So first of all...what?

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:03.180
How's that guy still around?

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:08.300
Well, Darth Maul is, if nothing else, a slippery
sucker, and fans of the animated series The

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:13.080
Clone Wars may recall that Maul survived being
cut in half and falling into that giant hole.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:17.500
Later, he got himself a fancy new pair of
robo-legs and became the leader of the criminal

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.680
organization called the Shadow Collective.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.780
So how does all this fit into Solo?

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.840
Well, clearly Maul is connected to the Crimson
Dawn, the criminal organization headed by

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.200
Dryden Vos in the film.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:31.440
Whether Maul pulls all the strings for the
Dawn or Vos simply reports to him is still

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.020
unclear, but Dryden Vos mentions that he can't
steal the coaxium from Kessel because he has

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.400
an alliance with the Pyke Syndicate, who run
the mine.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.440
In Star Wars canon, the Pyke Syndicate are
members of Maul's Shadow Collective, suggesting

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:47.160
that the Crimson Dawn is just another criminal
organization working for Maul's bigger gangster

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:47.660
group.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:48.820
Qi'ra

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:54.220
Similarly, the movie's ending makes it clear
that we haven't seen the last of Qi'ra.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:59.189
As one of Han's many betrayers, she certainly
has her place in Solo, but as an ally of Maul…

00:03:59.189 --> 00:04:01.380
well, things just got a lot more interesting.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:06.299
One theory has her becoming a Sith, but probably
that's not likely since Maul himself technically

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:07.460
isn't a Sith any more.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:11.680
But he still has all the training, and if
he took Qi'ra under his wing there are definitely

00:04:11.689 --> 00:04:13.180
a few things he could teach her.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:17.000
Combined with Qi'ra's knowledge of the Teras
Kasi fighting style - the same style The Last

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.420
Jedi's Praetorian guard used - that could
make her a formidable opponent in the future.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:22.880
Thanks for watching!

